<problem>
  <variantControl numVariants="1" />
<!-- <title>Secant and derivative of a quadratic, worksheet</title> -->

<setup>

<function name="f" variables="x" symbolic="true" applyStyleToLabel="true">5x^2
  <label><m>f(x)</m></label>
</function>
<math name="f_x_plus_h" simplify="full">$$f(x+h)</math>
<math name="h">h</math>
<math name="secant_slope_numerator" simplify="full" expand="true">$$f(x+$h )-$$f(x)</math>
<math name="neg_secant_slope_numerator" simplify="full" expand="true">-$$f(x+$h )+$$f(x)</math><math name="secant_slope" simplify="full" expand="true">$secant_slope_numerator /$h </math>
<math name="fprime" simplify="full">10x</math>
<math name="one">1</math>

</setup>


<p>Let <m>f(x)</m> be the quadratic function <m>f(x)=<math extend="$f.formula" /></m>.</p>


<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Calculate the slope of the secant line from <m>x</m> to <m>x+h</m>.</p>

<p> slope <m>= \biggl[</m>
  <mathInput name="mi1" />
<m> - \biggl(</m>
  <mathInput name="mi2" />
<m>\biggr)\biggr]\bigg/</m>
  <mathInput name="mi3" />
</p>
<answer matchPartial="true">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi1 $mi2 $mi3">
    <when>
      $mi1 = $f_x_plus_h
      and
      $mi2 = $f
      and
      $mi3 = $h
    </when>
  </award>
  <award>
    <when>
      $mi1 = $f
      and
      $mi2 = $f_x_plus_h
      and
      $mi3 = -$h
    </when>
  </award>
</answer>


<p>Simplify your expression by multiplying out the quadratic and canceling terms:
</p>
<p>

slope <m>= </m> 
  <mathInput name="mi21" />
<m>\bigg/</m>
  <mathInput name="mi22" />
</p>
<answer matchPartial="true" symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="numbers">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi21 $mi22">
    <when>
      $mi21=$secant_slope_numerator
      and
      $mi22=$h
    </when>
  </award>
    <award>
    <when>
      $mi21=$neg_secant_slope_numerator
      and
      $mi22=-$h
    </when>
  </award>
</answer>
<p>Cancel the <m>h</m> in the numerator and denominator to get your final answer:
</p>
<p>

secant slope = 
<answer>
<award>$secant_slope </award>
<award credit="0" name="limit_too_early">$fprime</award>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback condition="$limit_too_early">
  We are asking for the slope of the <em>secant</em> line, which is the line with a finite value of <m>h</m>.  Don't take the limit of <m>h \to 0</m> yet!
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Calculate an expression for the derivative <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m> by taking the limit as <m>h \to 0</m> of the secant slope calculated above.  Since you've canceled the <m>h</m> from the denominator, taking the limit is as simple as replacing <m>h</m> with zero.
</p>
  
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{df}{dx} = \lim_{h \to 0 } </m> 
  <mathInput name="mi31" />
 <m>=</m> 
  <mathInput name="mi32" />
</p>
<answer>
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi31 $mi32" matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      $mi31 = $secant_slope
      and
      $mi32 = $fprime
    </when>
  </award>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Sketch a graph of <m>f(x)</m> and its derivative <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>.  Pay attention to the places where <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m> is positive, zero, and negative.  Drag the <text extend="$p3.styleDescription" /> points so that the <text extend="$parab1.styleDescription" /> curve represents the graph of <m>f</m>. Drag the <text extend="$p1.styleDescription" /> points so that the <text extend="$line1.styleDescription" /> line represents the graph of <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>.
</p>

<graph size="large" aspectRatio="5/4" xmin="-4" xmax="4" ymin="-11" ymax="11" showNavigation="false" grid="1 1">

<point name="p1" x="-2" y="0">
<attractToGrid dx="0.5" />
</point>

<point name="p2" x="1" y="0">
<attractToGrid dx="0.5" />
</point>

<line name="line1" through="$p1 $p2" applyStyleToLabel="true">
  <label><m>\frac{df}{dx}</m></label>
</line>

<point stylenumber="2" name="p3" x="-1" y="0">
<attractToGrid dx="0.5" />
</point>

<point stylenumber="2" name="p4" x="0" y="0">
<attractToGrid dx="0.5" />
</point>

<point stylenumber="2" name="p5" x="2" y="0">
<attractToGrid dx="0.5" />
</point>

<parabola name="parab1" stylenumber="2" through="$p3 $p4 $p5" applyStyleToLabel="true">
  <label><m>f(x)</m></label>
</parabola>

</graph>

<p>Graph of function:
  <answer>
    <award><when>
      <math extend="$parab1.equation" isResponse="true" /> = <math>y = 5x^2</math>
    </when></award>
  </answer>
</p>
<p>Graph of derivative:
  <answer>
    <award><when>
      <math extend="$line1.equation" isResponse="true" /> = <math>y = 10x</math>
    </when></award>
  </answer>
</p>
</exercise>


<solution>


<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<md>
  <mrow>\text{secant slope}\amp = \frac{($f_x_plus_h ) - (<math extend="$f.formula" />) }{ $h  }</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp = \frac{ $secant_slope_numerator  }{ $h  }</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp = $secant_slope </mrow>
</md>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<me>\frac{df}{dx} = \lim_{h \to 0} $secant_slope  = $fprime </me>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
The graph of <m>f</m> (thick blue curve) and <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m> (thin red curve).

<graph size="large" aspectRatio="5/4" xmin="-4" xmax="4" ymin="-11" ymax="11" showNavigation="false" grid="1 1">

<point fixed="true">(0,0)</point>
<point fixed="true">(1,10)</point>
<line fixed="true" applyStyleToLabel="true">y=10x
  <label><m>\frac{df}{dx}</m></label>
</line>

<point stylenumber="2" fixed="true">(-1,5)</point>
<point stylenumber="2" fixed="true">(0,0)</point>
<point stylenumber="2" fixed="true">(1,5)</point>
<function fixed="true" extend="$f" stylenumber="2" symbolic="false" />

</graph>
</exercise>

</solution>


</problem>